## Claim
Text: "America went from 75,000 flu deaths last year in 2019 to almost 0 in 2020"
Claim date: October 26, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### How does the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) calculate the estimated annual number of influenza deaths compared to the actual number of laboratory-confirmed influenza deaths?
Answer: The CDC calculates the estimated annual number of influenza-associated deaths using statistical regression models based on national mortality data from death certificates (specifically looking at pneumonia, influenza, respiratory, and circulatory causes) combined with weekly viral surveillance data. This estimation method is used because relying on actual laboratory-confirmed influenza deaths would result in an undercount; as the CDC notes, "a diagnosis of influenza virus infection often is not confirmed with sensitive and specific laboratory diagnostics, particularly among older persons, and even when identified is rarely recorded on death certificates."

Source URL: https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/mm5933a1.htm

### Did public health measures implemented in the United States in 2020 to combat COVID-19, such as social distancing and mask-wearing, have a documented impact on the transmission and death rates of influenza?
Answer: According to the surveillance report, influenza activity in the United States began to decline in March 2020, which was "perhaps associated with community prevention measures for COVID-19." However, the search result does not explicitly document the direct impact of these measures on influenza death rates, and it notes that due to the pandemic, influenza surveillance data "may not represent an accurate reflection of influenza activity" and should be interpreted with caution.

Source URL: https://doh.wa.gov/sites/default/files/legacy/Documents/5100//420-100-FluUpdateSeason2020.pdf

### Is there any evidence that influenza deaths in the United States in 2020 were being systematically misclassified as COVID-19 deaths?
Answer: Based on the provided search result, there is no evidence that influenza deaths in the United States were being systematically misclassified as COVID-19 deaths. Instead, comparisons of overall mortality from all causes in 2020 showed an unusual increase in deaths, which "further confirms that the cases and mortality attributed to COVID-19 is not due to a mislabeling of 'normal' flu cases." Furthermore, the CDC recorded typical flu activity for the 2019-2020 season, estimating that the flu accounted for 24,000 to 62,000 deaths in the U.S. during that period.

Source URL: https://healthfeedback.org/claimreview/2020-has-seen-a-number-of-flu-cases-and-deaths-comparable-to-previous-years/

